    "name":  "Obstructive sleep apnoea",
    "module":  "respiratory",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Obstructive sleep apnoea (OSA) is a sleep-related breathing disorder characterised by repetitive episodes of complete (apnoea) or partial (hypopnoea) upper airway obstruction during sleep, causing intermittent hypoxia and sleep fragmentation. It affects approximately 10–30% of adults, is significantly underdiagnosed, and is associated with hypertension, cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, and road traffic accidents from excessive daytime sleepiness. The severity is classified by the apnoea-hypopnoea index (AHI): mild 5–14/h, moderate 15–29/h, severe ≥30/h. Diagnosis requires polysomnography or home sleep testing. First-line treatment is continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP); weight loss, positional therapy, and mandibular advancement devices are alternatives.",
